      "rationale": "All column variants realize the published sliding poles and max out at the same 14.17-14.31% entry-1 deviation vs Eq. (62) (spread 0.14 pp, tied for practical purposes); computed g=9.81 matrices are the first-principles choice per the project README and are adopted. Printed-matrix b=column wins entry 2 (-147.82 vs published -147.918) but loses entries 3-4; the differences are inside the rounding noise of Eqs. (25)-(26)."
    },
    "key_finding": "No variant reproduces Eq. (62) entry 1 (theta coefficient): the CCF design gives -2.197 vs the published -1.924 (14% off) while entries 2-4 agree to 0.6-1.7%. Every candidate that realizes the Eq. (60-61) sliding poles shares this entry-1 gap, and the published Eq. (62)-(63) pair is internally consistent (<=0.5%) only with b = Bd[:,0] and Sb = 1 -- exactly what the CCF construction produces automatically. The residual entry-1 discrepancy is a transcription/derivation artifact of the paper, documented here as the checkpoint's honest finding."
  },
